Thanks [~accountid:6363e99c76b91b62562fd8f4] - responses are below.

{quote}DOB/Nationality - *absolutely necessary*{quote}

Understood.

{quote}Gender - as a company, Bolt offers "women4women" or "womendriver" category, where we promise users they will be picked up by a female driver. That is not possible to do if we don't gather and process this data. This is a global issue and it should be included in the T&Cs or the Privacy Notice on a Global level.{quote}

I have recently been advising on the W4W category, and had understood that gender data was being extracted from the user’s ID provided to Bolt. Is the team that has been developing this feature aware that you intend to also ask the user to separately confirm their gender in the way you are suggesting? 

{quote}We would like to proceed with the changes to our local registration flows, as soon as possible and we are aligned on this with central ops. stakeholders.{quote}

I fully appreciate this. However, as flagged, *unless there is a* +*pressing*+ *need to develop unique processes on a local level, we generally advise against this*. We would prefer a unified process globally rather than bespoke solutions on a per-jurisdiction level. Bespoke solutions complicate business flows, affect the ability to do urgent fact-finding where stakeholders are not fully aligned, and prevents us from being able to carefully regulate exactly what Bolt is doing at a given time. 

Based on the description you have provided, it sounds like we already collect this data but the proposal is just to change _how_ we collect this data for the sake of operational efficiency. This appears to fall more into the ‘nice to have’ category rather than being strictly necessary.

If the business feels the need to go ahead with this, then that is a decision for the business to make. However, the view from our side is that we would advise +*against*+ implementing bespoke local solutions like this one. This is not a Privacy law issue, but a process management point.

{quote}What is the potential timeline on your side to update T&Cs / Privacy Policy? If this is substantial (i.e. months, not days or weeks) we would like to understand the risks if we were to proceed with these changes before the documentation is updated.{quote}

We are still updating the Privacy Notice and expect it to be ready over the coming months. Transparency gaps like this one can result in fines from regulators / complaints from users, although seeing as the data here is not particularly sensitive we consider this to be low risk. Since this is a known compliance gap (and is an issue in other areas), the Privacy Notice not being fully up-to-date would not act as a blocker to implementing the changes to the local registration flows (if the business chose to go ahead with this).

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*CONCLUSION*: From a Privacy law POV, implementing these changes is low risk and does not raise any material issues. However, the issue here is that we always advise taking a unified approach globally (unless it is strictly necessary to take a different approach) - since these changes do not seem to be strictly necessary, +*we would advise against only doing this on the local level*+. If the business decides to proceed with this then it is free to do so, but it may create process management issues in the long term.
{panel}

Happy to discuss 🙂

